{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '158,218p' test/dao/space/query/execution_test.cljc; sed -n '683,760p' docs/design/dao.space.query.datomic-compat.md; sed -n '20,95p' docs/design/datom.world.md\"","aggregated_output":"      (is (= :step-budget-exhausted\n             (:kind (ex/outcome (-> s0 (ex/charge :candidate) (ex/charge :candidate)\n                                    (ex/charge :candidate)))))))))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; The one-in-flight read guarantee\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n(defn- recording-store\n  \"Read thunks that log which read ran, with the clock advancing past the\n   deadline during read number `slow`. Test-local scaffolding.\"\n  [n slow deadline]\n  (let [log (volatile! [])\n        clock (volatile! 0)\n        driver (ex/fn-driver (fn [] @clock))\n        reads (mapv (fn [i]\n                      (fn []\n                        (vswap! log conj i)\n                        (when (= i slow) (vreset! clock (+ deadline 1)))\n                        i))\n                    (range n))]\n    {:driver driver :reads reads :log log}))\n\n\n(deftest at-most-one-read-runs-past-the-deadline\n  (testing \"expiry during read 2: that read completes, reads 3.. never start\"\n    (let [{:keys [driver reads log]} (recording-store 6 2 100)\n          [s vals] (ex/run-reads (ex/start {:deadline 100}) driver reads)]\n      (is (= :timeout (:kind (ex/outcome s))))\n      (is (= [0 1 2] @log) \"exactly one in-flight read beyond the deadline\")\n      (is (= [0 1] vals) \"no partial value from the overshooting read is delivered\")))\n  (testing \"expiry before the first read: no read starts\"\n    (let [{:keys [driver reads log]} (recording-store 3 -1 100)\n          start (ex/start {:deadline 0})\n          [s _] (ex/run-reads start driver reads)]\n      (is (= :timeout (:kind (ex/outcome s))))\n      (is (empty? @log) \"an expired state starts no read\")))\n  (testing \"no expiry: every read runs\"\n    (let [{:keys [driver reads log]} (recording-store 4 -1 100)\n          [s vals] (ex/run-reads (ex/start {:deadline 100}) driver reads)]\n      (is (not (ex/terminal? s)))\n      (is (= [0 1 2 3] @log vals))))\n  (testing \"a terminal state never starts a read\"\n    (let [{:keys [driver reads log]} (recording-store 2 -1 100)\n          dead (ex/charge (ex/charge (ex/start {:max-steps 1}) :candidate) :candidate)\n          [s _] (ex/run-reads dead driver reads)]\n      (is (= :step-budget-exhausted (:kind (ex/outcome s))))\n      (is (empty? @log)))))\n\n\n(deftest ownership-is-not-touched\n  (testing \"the machinery only reads the values it is handed\"\n    (let [source {:dao.space.query/relation [[1 :a 2]]}\n          before (pr-str source)\n          {:keys [driver]} (counting-driver 500 0)\n          _ (ex/finish (ex/start {:deadline 500}) driver)\n          _ (ex/run-reads (ex/start {:deadline 500}) driver [(fn [] source)])]\n      (is (= before (pr-str source)) \"inputs are neither opened, closed nor changed\"))))\n\n\n## Implementation sequence and ownership\n\nRetain phase IDs from the initial plan for traceability. Execute in this\norder:\n\n**0 → 1 → 4 → 2 → 3 → 5**\n\nPhase 1 prioritizes representative finite pull and list-surface cases.\nPhase 4 moves ahead of aggregate/rule expansion because backend query\nbounds are essential to the web-app consumer. This changes scheduling,\nnot the accepted feature boundary.\n\n| Phase | Ownership and files | Acceptance criteria | Implementation agent-hours |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| 0 — Contract and fixtures | Architect: this document. Engineer: corpus, README/errata/provenance, fixture namespace, conformance harness, oracle runner and dev-only dependency alias. | Every contract point classified; no fabricated oracle evidence; fixtures load portably; disputed behavior has explicit oracle cases; pending cases reported honestly. | 3–5 |\n| 1 — Adapter boundary and view workloads | Engineer: `src/cljc/dao/space/query/datomic.cljc`, `test/dao/space/query/datomic_test.cljc`, consumer fixtures and examples. | Both verified public arities; safe strings; argument/option validation; realized tuple/return-map output; pagination defaults/boundaries; finite pull profile validated; unsupported features refused; existing core result tests unchanged. | 4–7 |\n| 4 — Bounded execution | Engineer: `src/cljc/dao/space/query/execution.cljc`, core integration, adapter driver, execution tests, and the minimal generic traversal seam needed for read checkpoints. | One threaded invocation state; normative unit accounting; checked forced pass; per-read guarantee; deterministic expiry tests; one clock smoke per host; minimal error context; no input ownership change; legacy API unchanged. | 12–22 |\n| 2 — Deterministic language coverage | Engineer: `query.cljc`, aggregate/numeric tests and adapter tests. | Six specified aggregate capabilities added; arities and parameters preserved; empty/grouped/`:with` cases; abbreviated current-fact patterns; special-form edges; three-host numeric-domain agreement. | 6–10 |\n| 3 — Rules and semantic conformance | Engineer: core rules and `test/dao/space/query_rules_test.cljc`; corpus expansion. | Required-bound heads; left/right/mutual finite positive recursion; cycles, alternatives and unbound entry calls; immutable tabling/fixed-point correction where necessary; unsupported recursion through negation refused; fresh-value work bounded by Phase 4; existing VM rules pass. | 8–14 |\n| 5 — Qualification and integration | Engineer: complete conformance, counters, ownership and downstream tests. Architect: final matrix and status headers. | Supported cases verified; differences/refusals executable; read budgets hold; downstream callers pass; no remaining unverified claim inside the qualified exact domain; docs match implementation. | 5–8 |\n\nEach phase must leave master testable. Land tests with their implementing\nslice; proposed/pending cases must not make unrelated lanes fail or appear\nas passing compatibility cases.\n\nPhase 4 may change only generic traversal machinery needed for the stated\nguarantee. It does not authorize a storage-format redesign or query-aware\nbackend. That seam requires architecture review before landing.\n\n### Downstream regression gates\n\nProtect:\n\n- `yin.vm` query-based extraction and rule workloads.\n- `yin.vm.ledger`.\n- Linker publication and semantic scanning.\n- `yin.repl.query` and its bounded-answer behavior.\n- `dao.space.schema`.\n- `dao.space.dht`, whose baseline `dht.cljc:1498` applies core `q` over a\n  current view of a loaded published index.\n- `dao.space.transact` realization paths using `match` and `current`,\n  including baseline lines 60, 80, 282, 304, 334 and 365.\n\nThe new adapter must not be required by legacy callers. Shared execution\nor realization changes must still pass those callers' existing tests.\n\nA Mode 1 consumer test should verify explicit session parameters,\ncurrent-source selection and query failure without session advancement.\nThe established dao.star integration gates remain authoritative for\nfact-before-UI ordering; query tests must not substitute a mock proof for\nthe actual router/driver protocol.\n\n### Effort and routing\n\nImplementation subtotal remains **38–66 agent-hours**.\nIndependent review and remediation add **6–10 agent-hours**.\nTotal remains **44–76 agent-hours**.\n\nScheduling alone does not change the estimate. The detailed checked-read\nseam and recursive completeness work are the largest risks and should be\nresolved early within their phase ranges. Re-estimate if investigation\nshows a broader traversal refactor is necessary.\n\nAssume one `claude-opus-5.5` engineering lane, independent\n`gemini-4-argon` implementation review, and GPT architecture sign-off for\nboundary changes. Fable may independently review this GPT-authored\ndesign, but is not a different-family reviewer of Opus implementation.\nCodex remains in architecture under the current roster.\n\nAt eight active implementation hours per working day, allow roughly\n6–10 working days plus scheduling/backend-availability delays. These are\nengineering estimates, not measured model throughput guarantees.\n\nPhases 0–1 are a 7–12-hour initial milestone with explicit exclusions.\nThey are not web-app backend qualification while execution bounds or\noracle evidence remain absent.\n\n## Invariants and portability\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/dao/space/query/datomic.cljc'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/dao/space/query/datomic.cljc'","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.space.query.datomic\n  \"The Datomic Client query adapter boundary\n   (docs/design/dao.space.query.datomic-compat.md, Architecture and\n   ownership).\n\n   `q` takes the two public Client call shapes, `(q arg-map)` and\n   `(q query & args)`, validates and normalizes them, translates database\n   pattern syntax, runs core `dao.space.query/q` + `collect` over the\n   caller's immutable source values, applies `:offset`/`:limit` to the\n   realized rows, and returns ordinary data.\n\n   The adapter borrows its sources: it opens, closes, publishes and writes\n   nothing, keeps no registry, no cache, no atom, no dynamic var and reads\n   no clock. Core evaluation never sees a Client option name.\n\n   Refused loudly (never silently ignored): the db-first call shape, raw\n   vectors/maps at source positions, unknown options, `:timeout` (until\n   the execution-bounds phase), `:ret`, `:io-context`, `:query-stats`,\n   four-/five-position history patterns, rest patterns, parameterized\n   aggregate syntax, unvalidated pull syntax (recursion, reverse\n   attributes, dynamic or source-qualified patterns).\n\n   Numeric limitation (corpus errata E1): core builds its final result set\n   with host `=`, so content-distinct numerics that compare equal on a host\n   (1.0M and 1.00M, 0.0 and -0.0 on the JVM) collapse to one row before\n   this adapter sees them; its vector output cannot recover them.\n\n   Errors are ex-info with `:dao.space.query.datomic/error` and\n   `:cognitect.anomalies/category`, carrying minimal context only (option,\n   variable, a bounded clause fragment). Core exception data, which can\n   embed bindings, is never copied; only a genuine evaluator or source\n   failure is kept as `ex-cause`.\"\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.space.query :as query]\n            #?(:cljd [clojure.edn :as edn]\n               :clj [clojure.edn :as edn]\n               :cljs [cljs.reader :as reader])))\n\n\n;; ==========================================================================\n;; Errors\n;; ==========================================================================\n\n(def ^:private categories\n  {:invalid-request :cognitect.anomalies/incorrect\n   :unsupported :cognitect.anomalies/unsupported\n   :evaluation-failed :cognitect.anomalies/fault\n   :source-failed :cognitect.anomalies/fault})\n\n\n(defn- fragment\n  \"A bounded printed fragment of a clause, never the whole input.\"\n  [x]\n  (let [s (pr-str x)]\n    (if (> (count s) 120) (str (subs s 0 120) \"...\") s)))\n\n\n(defn- fail\n  \"Throw the adapter error `kind` with a message and minimal context.\"\n  ([kind message context]\n   (throw (ex-info message\n                   (merge {:cognitect.anomalies/category (get categories kind)}\n                          context\n                          {::error kind}))))\n  ([kind message context cause]\n   (throw (ex-info message\n                   (merge {:cognitect.anomalies/category (get categories kind)}\n                          context\n                          {::error kind})\n                   cause))))\n\n\n;; ==========================================================================\n;; Safe EDN string reading\n;; ==========================================================================\n\n(def ^:private max-string-length 100000)\n(def ^:private max-nesting 64)\n\n\n(defn- scan-string\n  \"Refuse, before any reader runs, what a safe profile must not read:\n   tagged literals, reader conditionals, evaluation, over-long or too deeply\n   nested text. Returns nil when acceptable, else [kind message].\"\n  [s]\n  (if (> (count s) max-string-length)\n    [:invalid-request \"query string is too long\"]\n    (loop [i 0 in-str? false esc? false comment? false depth 0]\n      (if (>= i (count s))\n        nil\n        (let [c (nth s i)]\n          (cond\n            comment? (recur (inc i) in-str? false (not= c \\newline) depth)\n            esc? (recur (inc i) in-str? false false depth)\n            in-str? (recur (inc i) (not= c \\\") (= c \\\\) false depth)\n            (= c \\\") (recur (inc i) true false false depth)\n            (= c \\;) (recur (inc i) false false true depth)\n            (= c \\\\) (recur (+ i 2) false false false depth)\n            (and (= c \\#) (< (inc i) (count s))\n                 (let [n (nth s (inc i))]\n                   (not (or (= n \\{) (= n \\_)))))\n            [:unsupported \"tagged literals and dispatch forms are not read\"]\n            (contains? #{\\( \\[ \\{} c)\n            (if (>= depth max-nesting)\n              [:invalid-request \"query string is nested too deeply\"]\n              (recur (inc i) false false false (inc depth)))\n            (contains? #{\\) \\] \\}} c) (recur (inc i) false false false (dec depth))\n            :else (recur (inc i) false false false depth)))))))\n\n\n(defn- read-edn\n  [s]\n  #?(:cljd (edn/read-string s)\n     :clj (edn/read-string {:readers {}\n                            :default (fn [_ _] (throw (ex-info \"tag\" {})))}\n                           s)\n     :cljs (reader/read-string {:readers {}\n                                :default (fn [_ _] (throw (ex-info \"tag\" {})))}\n                               s)))\n\n\n(defn- read-query-string\n  \"The single EDN form in s, read without evaluation or tag handlers.\"\n  [s]\n  (when-let [[kind message] (scan-string s)]\n    (fail kind message {:option :query}))\n  (let [forms (try (read-edn (str \"[\" s \"\\n]\"))\n                   (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n                     (fail :invalid-request \"query string is not readable EDN\"\n                           {:option :query})))]\n    (when-not (and (vector? forms) (= 1 (count forms)))\n      (fail :invalid-request \"query string must hold exactly one form\"\n            {:option :query}))\n    (first forms)))\n\n\n;; ==========================================================================\n;; Query normalization\n;; ==========================================================================\n\n(def ^:private sections #{:find :in :with :where :keys :syms :strs})\n\n\n(defn- sections-of-sequence\n  [form]\n  (loop [xs (seq form) result {} cur nil vals []]\n    (if-not xs\n      (if cur (assoc result cur vals) result)\n      (let [x (first xs)]\n        (cond\n          (contains? sections x)\n          (do (when (or (= cur x) (contains? result x))\n                (fail :invalid-request \"query section repeated\" {:clause x}))\n              (recur (next xs) (if cur (assoc result cur vals) result) x []))\n          (nil? cur)\n          (fail :invalid-request \"query must start with a section keyword\"\n                {:clause (fragment x)})\n          :else (recur (next xs) result cur (conj vals x)))))))\n\n\n(defn- query-map\n  \"The validated query map of a map, a sequential form or an EDN string.\"\n  [form]\n  (let [form (if (string? form) (read-query-string form) form)\n        m (cond\n            (map? form)\n            (do (doseq [k (keys form)]\n                  (when-not (contains? sections k)\n                    (fail :invalid-request \"unknown query section\"\n                          {:clause (fragment k)})))\n                (into {} (map (fn [[k v]]\n                                (when-not (sequential? v)\n                                  (fail :invalid-request \"query section must be a sequence\"\n                                        {:clause k}))\n                                [k (vec v)]))\n                      form))\n            (sequential? form) (sections-of-sequence form)\n            :else (fail :invalid-request\n                        \"query must be a map, a vector/list or an EDN string\"\n                        {:option :query}))]\n    (when (empty? (:find m))\n      (fail :invalid-request \"query requires a non-empty :find\" {:clause :find}))\n    (assoc m :where (vec (:where m)))))\n\n\n;; ==========================================================================\n;; Pattern translation\n;; ==========================================================================\n\n(defn- db-sym?\n  [x]\n  (and (symbol? x) (= \\$ (first (name x)))))\n\n\n(defn- pad-pattern\n  [clause]\n  (let [items (vec clause)\n        src? (db-sym? (first items))\n        body (if src? (subvec items 1) items)\n        n (count body)]\n    (cond\n      (some #(= '& %) body)\n      (fail :unsupported \"rest patterns are outside the Client profile\"\n            {:clause (fragment clause)})\n      (zero? n) (fail :invalid-request \"empty data pattern\" {:clause (fragment clause)})\n      (> n 3) (fail :unsupported\n                    \"four-/five-position history patterns are outside this profile\"\n                    {:clause (fragment clause)})\n      :else (into (if src? [(first items)] [])\n                  (concat body (repeat (- 3 n) '_))))))\n\n\n(declare translate-clause)\n\n\n(defn- translate-clauses\n  [clauses]\n  (mapv translate-clause clauses))\n\n\n(defn- rebuild\n  \"A (head [prefix] children...) list with translated children.\"\n  [head more children]\n  (apply list head (concat more (translate-clauses children))))\n\n\n(defn- translate-clause\n  [clause]\n  (cond\n    (vector? clause)\n    (if (seq? (first clause)) clause (pad-pattern clause))\n\n    (seq? clause)\n    (let [[h & more] clause\n          src (when (db-sym? (first more)) [(first more)])\n          more (if src (rest more) more)]\n      (cond\n        (contains? #{'not 'or 'and} h) (rebuild h src more)\n        (contains? #{'not-join 'or-join} h)\n        (do (when-not (vector? (first more))\n              (fail :invalid-request \"join form requires a variable vector\"\n                    {:clause (fragment clause)}))\n            (rebuild h (concat src [(first more)]) (rest more)))\n        :else clause))\n\n    :else (fail :invalid-request \"unrecognized where clause\"\n                {:clause (fragment clause)})))\n\n\n(defn- translate-rules\n  [rules]\n  (when-not (sequential? rules)\n    (fail :invalid-request \"rules input must be a vector of rules\" {:option :args}))\n  (mapv (fn [rule]\n          (when-not (and (sequential? rule) (seq rule))\n            (fail :invalid-request \"malformed rule\" {:clause (fragment rule)}))\n          (into [(first rule)] (translate-clauses (rest rule))))\n        rules))\n\n\n;; ==========================================================================\n;; Find validation (aggregates and finite pull)\n;; ==========================================================================\n\n(def ^:private core-aggregates\n  #{'count 'count-distinct 'sum 'min 'max 'avg})\n\n\n(def ^:private later-aggregates\n  #{'distinct 'median 'variance 'stddev 'rand 'sample})\n\n\n(declare check-pull-pattern)\n\n\n(defn- check-attr-options\n  [element]\n  (let [[attr & opts] element]\n    (when-not (and (keyword? attr) (not= \\_ (first (name attr))))\n      (fail :unsupported \"pull attribute options need a forward keyword attribute\"\n            {:clause (fragment element)}))\n    (when (odd? (count opts))\n      (fail :invalid-request \"pull attribute options must be key/value pairs\"\n            {:clause (fragment element)}))\n    (doseq [[k v] (partition 2 opts)]\n      (when-not (contains? #{:default :as :limit} k)\n        (fail :unsupported \"pull attribute option is outside the admitted profile\"\n              {:option k}))\n      (when (and (= :limit k) (not (and (integer? v) (not (neg? v)))))\n        (fail :invalid-request \"pull :limit must be a nonnegative integer\"\n              {:option :limit})))))\n\n\n(defn- check-pull-pattern\n  [pattern]\n  (cond\n    (symbol? pattern)\n    (fail :unsupported \"dynamic pull patterns are outside the admitted profile\" {})\n    (not (vector? pattern))\n    (fail :invalid-request \"pull pattern must be a vector\" {:clause (fragment pattern)})\n    :else\n    (doseq [e pattern]\n      (cond\n        (= '* e) nil\n        (keyword? e)\n        (when (= \\_ (first (name e)))\n          (fail :unsupported \"reverse pull attributes are not admitted\"\n                {:clause (fragment e)}))\n        (vector? e) (check-attr-options e)\n        (map? e)\n        (do (when-not (= 1 (count e))\n              (fail :unsupported \"one nested attribute per pull map\"\n                    {:clause (fragment e)}))\n            (let [[attr sub] (first e)]\n              (when-not (and (keyword? attr) (not= \\_ (first (name attr))))\n                (fail :unsupported \"nested pull needs a forward keyword attribute\"\n                      {:clause (fragment e)}))\n              (when (or (= '... sub) (integer? sub))\n                (fail :unsupported \"recursive pull is not admitted\"\n                      {:clause (fragment e)}))\n              (check-pull-pattern sub)))\n        (seq? e)\n        (fail :unsupported\n              \"list-form pull options are not read; use the vector form [attr :default x]\"\n              {:clause (fragment e)})\n        :else (fail :invalid-request \"malformed pull pattern\" {:clause (fragment e)})))))\n\n\n(defn- find-elements\n  [find]\n  (let [f (first find)]\n    (if (vector? f) f find)))\n\n\n(defn- check-find\n  [find]\n  (doseq [el (find-elements find)]\n    (when (seq? el)\n      (let [h (first el)]\n        (cond\n          (= 'pull h)\n          (do (when-not (= 3 (count el))\n                (fail :unsupported\n                      \"source-qualified or malformed pull is outside the admitted profile\"\n                      {:clause (fragment el)}))\n              (check-pull-pattern (nth el 2)))\n          (or (contains? later-aggregates h) (> (count el) 2))\n          (fail :unsupported \"aggregate or parameterized aggregate is not available yet\"\n                {:variable (fragment el)})\n          (not (contains? core-aggregates h))\n          (fail :invalid-request \"unknown aggregate\" {:variable (fragment el)}))))))\n\n\n;; ==========================================================================\n;; Inputs\n;; ==========================================================================\n\n(defn- check-inputs\n  \"Validate :args against :in before evaluation. Source positions come from\n   :in only; every other argument stays ordinary data, even a map or vector.\n   Rules passed at `%` get the same pattern translation as :where.\"\n  [in args]\n  (when-not (sequential? args)\n    (fail :invalid-request \":args must be a vector\" {:option :args}))\n  (let [args (vec args)]\n    (when-not (= (count in) (count args))\n      (fail :invalid-request \"number of :args must match :in\" {:option :args}))\n    (mapv (fn [pattern arg]\n            (cond\n              (db-sym? pattern)\n              (if (query/value? arg)\n                arg\n                (fail :invalid-request\n                      (str \"source input must be a dao.space query value; wrap raw \"\n                           \"data with dao.space.query/relation, fact-relation, \"\n                           \"current or history\")\n                      {:variable (fragment pattern)}))\n              (= '% pattern) (translate-rules arg)\n              :else arg))\n          in args)))\n\n\n;; ==========================================================================\n;; Options and pagination\n;; ==========================================================================\n\n(def ^:private known-options #{:query :args :offset :limit})\n\n\n(def ^:private refused-options #{:timeout :ret :io-context :query-stats :stats})\n\n\n(defn- check-options\n  [arg-map]\n  (doseq [k (keys arg-map)]\n    (cond\n      (contains? known-options k) nil\n      (contains? refused-options k)\n      (fail :unsupported (str \"option \" k \" is outside this profile\") {:option k})\n      :else (fail :unsupported \"unknown option is not ignored\"\n                  {:option (if (keyword? k) k (fragment k))})))\n  (let [offset (get arg-map :offset 0)\n        limit (get arg-map :limit -1)]\n    (when-not (and (integer? offset) (not (neg? offset)))\n      (fail :invalid-request \":offset must be a nonnegative integer\" {:option :offset}))\n    (when-not (and (integer? limit) (or (= -1 limit) (not (neg? limit))))\n      (fail :invalid-request \":limit must be a nonnegative integer or -1\"\n            {:option :limit}))\n    [offset limit]))\n\n\n(defn- paginate\n  [result spec offset limit]\n  (let [paged? (or (pos? offset) (not= -1 limit))]\n    (cond\n      (contains? #{:scalar :tuple} spec)\n      (do (when paged?\n            (fail :unsupported \"offset/limit require a relation or collection find\"\n                  {:option (if (pos? offset) :offset :limit)}))\n          result)\n      :else\n      (let [rows (cond->> (seq result)\n                   (pos? offset) (drop offset)\n                   (not= -1 limit) (take limit))]\n        (vec rows)))))\n\n\n;; ==========================================================================\n;; Execution\n;; ==========================================================================\n\n(defn- spec-of\n  [find]\n  (let [f (first find)]\n    (cond (and (vector? f) (some #(= '... %) f)) :coll\n          (vector? f) :tuple\n          (some #(= (symbol \".\") %) find) :scalar\n          :else :relation)))\n\n\n(defn- classify-core-failure\n  \"Rethrow a core failure under the adapter taxonomy. Core ex-data can embed\n   bindings, so ordinary core refusals keep only their message; a genuine\n   fault or source failure keeps its cause.\"\n  [e]\n  (let [data (ex-data e)\n        category (:cognitect.anomalies/category data)]\n    (cond\n      (::error data) (throw e)\n      category (fail :source-failed \"source read failed\"\n                     {:cognitect.anomalies/category category} e)\n      (some? data)\n      (let [msg (or (ex-message e) \"query refused\")]\n        (if (str/includes? msg \"not implemented\")\n          (fail :unsupported msg {})\n          (fail :invalid-request msg {})))\n      :else (fail :evaluation-failed \"query evaluation failed\" {} e))))\n\n\n(defn- run\n  [m args]\n  (let [in (vec (or (seq (:in m)) ['$]))\n        _ (check-find (:find m))\n        where (translate-clauses (:where m))\n        bound (check-inputs in args)\n        core-query (assoc m :in in :where where)]\n    (try\n      (query/collect (apply query/q core-query bound))\n      (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n        (classify-core-failure e)))))\n\n\n(defn- execute\n  [form args offset limit]\n  (let [m (query-map form)\n        result (run m args)]\n    (paginate result (if (or (:keys m) (:syms m) (:strs m))\n                       :relation\n                       (spec-of (:find m)))\n              offset limit)))\n\n\n(defn- db-first?\n  [x]\n  (or (query/value? x)\n      (and (map? x) (contains? x :relation) (contains? x :status))))\n\n\n(defn q\n  \"Datomic Client `q`: `(q arg-map)` with `:query`, `:args`, `:offset`,\n   `:limit`, or `(q query & args)`. `query` is a map, a vector/list or an\n   EDN string. Returns realized data; see the namespace docstring for the\n   refusals and the error taxonomy.\"\n  ([arg-map]\n   (cond\n     (db-first? arg-map)\n     (fail :unsupported \"db-first q is not the public Client call shape\" {})\n\n     (and (map? arg-map) (contains? arg-map :query))\n     (let [[offset limit] (check-options arg-map)]\n       (execute (:query arg-map) (get arg-map :args []) offset limit))\n\n     (or (string? arg-map) (sequential? arg-map)\n         (and (map? arg-map) (some #(contains? arg-map %) [:find :where :in])))\n     (execute arg-map [] 0 -1)\n\n     :else (fail :invalid-request \"q requires an arg-map or a query\" {})))\n  ([query-or-db & args]\n   (if (db-first? query-or-db)\n     (fail :unsupported \"db-first q is not the public Client call shape\" {})\n     (execute query-or-db (vec args) 0 -1))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '120,205p' src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc; sed -n '345,430p' src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc; sed -n '1900,1995p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; sed -n '2070,2115p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; sed -n '350,395p' docs/design/dao.space.query.datomic-compat.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '120,205p' src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc; sed -n '345,430p' src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc; sed -n '1900,1995p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; sed -n '2070,2115p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc; sed -n '350,395p' docs/design/dao.space.query.datomic-compat.md\"","aggregated_output":"  (mapv vec (bound-datoms (flatten-datoms elements) as-of)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Entity-map relation normalization (explicit d3 projection)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- entity-map->d3\n  [m]\n  (when-not (map? m)\n    (throw (ex-info \"entity-map-relation requires entity maps\" {:entity m})))\n  (when-not (contains? m :db/id)\n    (throw (ex-info \"raw entity-map source requires an explicit :db/id\"\n                    {:entity m})))\n  (let [e (:db/id m)]\n    (into [] (keep (fn [[a v]] (when (not= a :db/id) [e a v]))) m)))\n\n\n(defn- entity-maps->d3\n  [maps]\n  (into [] (mapcat entity-map->d3) maps))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Query values: relation, view, opened published index (Decision 2)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn value?\n  \"True for the query values q/match/pull take as database inputs: a\n   relation value, a datom view, or an opened published index. Raw vectors\n   and raw maps answer false.\"\n  [x]\n  (and (map? x)\n       (or (contains? x :dao.space.query/relation)\n           (contains? x :dao.space.query/view)\n           (contains? x :dao.space.query/published))))\n\n\n(defn relation\n  \"An in-memory relation value. Its retained contents are an arbitrary,\n   mixed-dimensional tuple collection; arity never selects an\n   interpretation. This is the replacement for the direct raw-vector input.\"\n  [tuples]\n  {:dao.space.query/relation (vec tuples)})\n\n\n(defn entity-map-relation\n  \"An entity-map relation value: a bounded relation of entity maps,\n   projected explicitly to [e a v] facts on the read side. The read side is\n   pure and never mints an entity id, so every map must carry :db/id.\"\n  [maps]\n  ;; Normalize at the explicit constructor boundary. The resulting value is\n  ;; the same generic relation value q consumes; no second transport type\n  ;; or opening path is needed merely because the input syntax was maps.\n  (relation (entity-maps->d3 maps)))\n\n\n(defn fact-relation\n  \"The explicit current-fact relation: d3 rows that are already\n   current-state facts, so the fact index is built for them without a d5\n   pass. dao.space.schema builds these directly.\"\n  [tuples]\n  (assoc (relation tuples) :fact? true))\n\n\n(defn- snapshot-value?\n  [x]\n  (and (map? x) (contains? x :relation) (contains? x :status)))\n\n\n(defn- db-source\n  \"Normalize a current/history source. Query values and snapshots pass\n   through (a snapshot contributes its relation); everything else — raw\n   maps and vectors included — throws.\"\n  [source]\n  (cond\n    (value? source) source\n    (snapshot-value? source) (:relation source)\n    :else\n    (throw (ex-info \"query db input must be a relation value, a datom view, or an opened published index; raw vectors and maps are rejected\"\n                    {:input source}))))\n\n\n(defn- view-value\n  [kind source as-of]\n  (cond-> {:dao.space.query/view kind, :source source}\n             :read (:read step)}))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Realize a db-value: value tag -> {::relation ::fact-index ::indexes}\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- relation->fact-index\n  [relation]\n  (index/index-datoms (mapv (fn [tuple]\n                              [(nth tuple 0) (nth tuple 1)\n                               (nth tuple 2) 0 datom/default-op])\n                            relation)))\n\n\n(defn- force-relation\n  \"Force a relation if it is delayed (e.g. from a lazy covered-index\n   realization) or return it directly. Memoization belongs to the delay:\n   forcing is therefore at most one walk, but this function itself neither\n   creates nor guarantees non-nilness.\"\n  [relation]\n  (if (delay? relation) @relation relation))\n\n\n(declare realize-db-value!)\n\n\n(defn- realize-datom-view\n  \"Interpret a current/history view inside the query layer. The nested\n   source is another query value; a current view with no as-of over an\n   opened published index routes through the restored covered sets, every\n   other combination resolves the source's rows.\"\n  [d]\n  (let [kind (:dao.space.query/view d)\n        as-of (:as-of d)\n        {src-relation ::relation, src-indexes ::indexes}\n        (realize-db-value! (:source d))]\n    (if (and (= :current kind) (nil? as-of) src-indexes)\n      {::relation (delay (d5->current-facts (force-relation src-relation) nil)),\n       ::fact-index src-indexes}\n      (let [forced-rel (force-relation src-relation)\n            rows (case kind\n                   :current (d5->current-facts forced-rel as-of)\n                   :history (d5->history-rows forced-rel as-of))]\n        {::relation rows,\n         ::fact-index (when (= :current kind)\n                        (relation->fact-index rows))}))))\n\n\n(defn ^:no-doc realize-db-value!\n  \"Turn one db-value into an immutable tuple relation plus, for current\n   fact views, an in-memory fact index. Opens nothing, closes nothing.\"\n  [db-value]\n  (when-not (map? db-value)\n    (throw\n      (ex-info\n        \"query db input must be a relation value, a datom view, or an opened published index; raw vectors and maps are rejected\"\n        {:input db-value})))\n  (cond\n    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/relation)\n    (let [tuples (:dao.space.query/relation db-value)]\n      {::relation tuples,\n       ::fact-index (when (:fact? db-value) (relation->fact-index tuples))})\n\n    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/published)\n    (let [indexes (index/covered-indexes db-value)]\n      {::relation (:rows db-value),\n       ::indexes indexes,\n       ::fact-index nil})\n\n    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/view)\n    (realize-datom-view db-value)\n\n    :else\n    (throw\n      (ex-info\n        \"query db input must be a relation value, a datom view, or an opened published index; raw vectors and maps are rejected\"\n        {:input db-value}))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; match: Linda-style positional template (an ergonomic materializer)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private FREE ::free)\n\n        (let [i (search-last ks l from cmp)]\n          (if (== i -1)\n            (retreat (cons [node 0] stack)) ; all keys here > from\n            (cons [node i] stack)))))))\n\n\n(defn- stack-key\n  [stack]\n  (let [[leaf idx] (first stack)] (arr/aget (node-keys leaf) idx)))\n\n\n(defn- stack-seq\n  [stack to to-incl? cmp]\n  (lazy-seq (when stack\n              (let [k (stack-key stack)]\n                (when (or (nil? to)\n                          (let [d (cmp k to)]\n                            (or (neg? d) (and to-incl? (zero? d)))))\n                  (cons k (stack-seq (advance stack) to to-incl? cmp)))))))\n\n\n(defn- stack-rseq\n  [stack lo lo-incl? cmp]\n  (lazy-seq (when stack\n              (let [k (stack-key stack)]\n                (when (or (nil? lo)\n                          (let [d (cmp k lo)]\n                            (or (pos? d) (and lo-incl? (zero? d)))))\n                  (cons k (stack-rseq (retreat stack) lo lo-incl? cmp)))))))\n\n\n(defn- non-empty\n  [s]\n  (when (some? (first s)) s))\n\n\n(defn- slice*\n  \"Generalized ascending range with per-bound inclusivity (nil = unbounded).\n   The exclusivity knobs exist for cljd's ISorted bit-flag contract (§4);\n   the public `slice` is inclusive-inclusive, matching psset.\"\n  [^BTSet s from from-incl? to to-incl? cmp]\n  (when (pos? (.-cnt s))\n    (let [root (resident-root s)]\n      (when-let [stack (if (nil? from)\n                         (descend-leftmost nil root)\n                         (when-let [st (descend-from root from cmp)]\n                           (if (and (not from-incl?)\n                                    (zero? (cmp (stack-key st) from)))\n                             (advance st) ; distinct keys: skip at most one\n                             st)))]\n        (non-empty (stack-seq stack to to-incl? cmp))))))\n\n\n(defn- rslice*\n  \"Generalized descending range: iterates from the hi bound down to lo.\"\n  [^BTSet s hi hi-incl? lo lo-incl? cmp]\n  (when (pos? (.-cnt s))\n    (let [root (resident-root s)]\n      (when-let [stack (if (nil? hi)\n                         (descend-rightmost nil root)\n                         (when-let [st (descend-from-r root hi cmp)]\n                           (if (and (not hi-incl?)\n                                    (zero? (cmp (stack-key st) hi)))\n                             (retreat st)\n                             st)))]\n        (non-empty (stack-rseq stack lo lo-incl? cmp))))))\n\n\n(defn slice\n  \"Lazy ascending seq of all X with from <= X <= to (nil bound = unbounded);\n   nil when empty. Explicit-comparator arity per psset.\"\n  ([^BTSet s from to] (slice s from to (.-cmp s)))\n  ([^BTSet s from to cmp] (slice* s from true to true cmp)))\n\n\n(defn rslice\n  \"Lazy descending seq of all X with to <= X <= from, iterating from `from`\n   downward (nil bound = unbounded); nil when empty.\"\n  ([^BTSet s from to] (rslice s from to (.-cmp s)))\n  ([^BTSet s from to cmp] (rslice* s from true to true cmp)))\n\n\n(defn seq\n  \"Lazy ascending seq of the whole set; nil when empty.\"\n  [^BTSet s]\n  (slice s nil nil))\n\n\n(defn rseq\n  \"Lazy descending seq of the whole set; nil when empty.\"\n  [^BTSet s]\n  (rslice s nil nil))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Bulk build (from-sorted-array / from-sequential;\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Inspection helpers for the invariant test suite\n\n(defn node-keys-vec\n  \"Vector of the node's valid keys [0 ... len).\"\n  [node]\n  (let [ks (node-keys node)] (mapv #(arr/aget ks %) (range (node-len node)))))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Durability entry points (§5.1, Phase 4)\n\n(defn store-tree\n  \"Store the dirty subgraph, children before parents (Merkle by\n   construction); returns the root address. No-op returning the existing\n   address when the set is already stored (§4). An empty set stores as nil\n   (the existing convention: nil root address, walk of nil => ()).\"\n  [^BTSet s storage]\n  (when (pos? (.-cnt s)) (-store-tree! s storage)))\n\n\n(defn restore-tree\n  \"A lazy BTSet over `address`; nothing is fetched until traversed. `cnt`\n   is the element count from the manifest (§5.1 — psset's fault-the-tree\n   count sentinel is deliberately not ported). nil address => the empty\n   set (cnt must be 0). The tree adopts the storage's Settings (`-settings`),\n   which is how the manifest's branching factor reaches every restored\n   node — never construct the storage with defaults for a tree published\n   at another branching factor (§5.1).\"\n  [cmp address storage cnt]\n  (let [sett (-settings storage)]\n    (if (nil? address)\n      (BTSet. nil cmp nil storage (empty-root sett) 0 0 sett nil)\n      (BTSet. nil cmp address storage nil cnt 0 sett nil))))\n\n\n(defn walk-addresses\n  \"Call on-address with every segment key reachable from address, the\n   root's own address first; descend into a subtree only while on-address\n   returns truthy (psset's prune protocol — lets a GC mark phase skip\n   subtrees shared with already-marked versions). nil address => nothing.\"\n  [storage address on-address]\n  (letfn [(walk-node\n            [n]\n            (when (branch? n)\n              (let [as (node-addresses-arr n)\n## Normative execution bounds\n\nThis section incorporates review findings F1, F2, F4, F5, F6, F9 and F10.\n\n### State and driver\n\nUse generic execution machinery in:\n\n`src/cljc/dao/space/query/execution.cljc`\n\nOne invocation owns one immutable execution state containing progress,\nremaining work allowance, deadline data, phase and continuation state.\nNested evaluation threads and returns that state through explicit `ctx`\nplumbing, following the existing `::active-rules` precedent.\n\nPassing the same initial state independently to multiple branches is not\nthreading: each branch must receive the state returned by prior work.\nNegation, disjunction, rule calls, pull, grouping and source realization\nmust not reset allowances.\n\nNo dynamic variable, atom, namespace-level clock default, background\ntask, future or callback registration may carry invocation progress.\nA lazy closure capturing an old `ctx` must not restart accounting when\nforced.\n\nThe execution driver explicitly interprets progress/read requests and\nsupplies observations. A synchronous zero-argument `now-fn` may be a\ndriver-local capability: the driver calls it at specified checkpoints.\nIt is never registered for later invocation and never stored in global\nstate. Core evaluation consumes time readings/deadlines as data and does\nnot resolve a clock.\n\nThe adapter mints the absolute deadline at entry, before parsing and\nsource realization. Use a monotonic source where available, expressed in\na common duration unit. Wall-clock fallback must be documented as such;\nclock adjustment cannot be silently represented as monotonic behavior.\n\nLegacy `q` uses the same semantic machinery with an explicit unbounded,\nnever-expiring policy. No Client option name enters the core.\n\nFor web-app calls, the composition supplies a finite work allowance in\naddition to a deadline. The adapter may expose this as the explicitly\nnon-Client extension `:dao.space.query.datomic/max-steps`, translated to\ngeneric execution data. Its omission means no step cap; it does not\nenable an ambient default.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"The source confirms the STOP was warranted: `::active-rules` only passes scope downward; it cannot return consumed work. B-tree slice construction can also fetch several nodes before yielding a row. I’m choosing one state-passing evaluator, with bounded queries over fully materialized sources delivered first. Published-index bounds will be a separate Phase 4b; until then, bounded calls using published sources must refuse before reading them."}}
